{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Reuben. And Judah.</b> Any [word] that is mobile like zayin — [e.g.] זבולן — when one of the letters bet, vav, kaf, lamed is prefixed to it, the prefix letter ought to bear a mobile shewa. But there is no ability in the tongue to pronounce two mobile shewas in succession. Therefore they put a ḥirik under the prefix letter. For if bet is the prefix letter, they say בִּזבולן; if kaf or lamed, כִּזבולן, לִזבולן. And if vav, they pronounce a shureq instead of ḥirik, as though the vav were in the reading of alef — namely וּזבולן — for the word appears as the reading of alef-זבולן. But if the first letter were yod, as in יהודה — since yod is among the quiescent letters, they quiesce it, as in נִשָּׂאֵנוּ בְּיָהוּדָה (Isaiah 7:6) — [then] כִּיהודה, לִיהוּדָה נַחְשׁוֹן (Numbers 1:7), for now the yod is quiescent, unmentioned and unfelt on the tongue. And likewise with vav: וִיהוּדָה. Now the tribes are written here according to the order of their birth:"], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "Resolved Isaiah 7:6 citation from ישעי' ז' ו'; the word cited appears to be a partial or variant quotation of that verse rather than a direct phrase match."}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:30:42.113182479Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}